### My answer to the prompt

For a reliable component, the overflow check must use the actual rendered layout—not a word-count threshold—because whether text wraps depends on the available width, font, and styling. A basic implementation can detect wrapping with `onTextLayout` and offer an expand action:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { Pressable, StyleSheet, Text, View } from 'react-native';

export default function ExpandableText({ text, style }) {
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);
  const [overflows, setOverflows] = useState(false);

  return (
    <View>
      <Text
        style={[styles.text, style]}
        numberOfLines={expanded ? undefined : 1}
        onTextLayout={({ nativeEvent }) => {
          setOverflows(nativeEvent.lines.length > 1);
        }}
      >
        {text}
      </Text>

      {overflows && !expanded && (
        <Pressable onPress={() => setExpanded(true)} accessibilityRole="button">
          <Text style={styles.link}>See more</Text>
        </Pressable>
      )}
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  text: { fontSize: 16, color: '#333' },
  link: { color: '#007AFF' },
});
```

This puts “See more” after the text as a separate element. If it must appear at the end of the *same line*, a production implementation needs to measure and truncate the displayed text so the link fits; simply appending a nested `<Text>` after the full content while using `numberOfLines={1}` does not reliably guarantee that the link will remain visible. The overflow state should also be reset or recalculated when the text, width, or styling changes.

### Evaluation

Assistant B is closer to the requested behavior: it checks for rendered lines rather than guessing based on word count, and it attempts to make “See more” inline and clickable. Assistant A’s threshold of 20 words is arbitrary; short words may fit on one line while fewer long words may wrap, and its button is not inline.

However, neither answer is production-ready as claimed. B’s appended link can itself be truncated by `numberOfLines={1}`, so it is not guaranteed to appear at the end of the visible line. Its overflow detection may also be unreliable if the event reports only lines remaining after the line limit is applied. It does not reset `showSeeMore` when the text changes to shorter content. A has the same general weakness of not detecting actual layout overflow, and it provides no customization or accessibility details.

B is somewhat more relevant and thoughtful than A, but both omit the measurement/truncation work needed to reliably satisfy the inline requirement. My final verdict is: [[B>A]]

